# Gödel sentence

A **Gödel sentence** for a theory $T$ is a sentence $G$ of arithmetic that $T$
proves equivalent to the statement that $G$ is not provable in $T$. It is the
one example of a [[strange-loop]] in which every level, every crossing and the
return can be written down and checked, and it is the reference case against
which the discussion measured every other candidate.

## Description

Take a consistent, computably axiomatizable theory $T$ extending Robinson
arithmetic, with its provability predicate $\operatorname{Prov}_T$. The
[[diagonal-lemma]] applied to $\neg\operatorname{Prov}_T(x)$ gives a sentence
$G$ with

$$T\vdash G\leftrightarrow\neg\operatorname{Prov}_T(\ulcorner G\urcorner).$$

Read from outside, $G$ says "I am not provable in $T$". Read from inside, it
is a statement about natural numbers and nothing else. Both readings are
correct, and the loop consists in their being the same sentence.

What follows depends on what is assumed about $T$.

| Assumption on $T$ | Conclusion |
|---|---|
| consistent | $T$ does not prove $G$ |
| $\omega$-consistent, or merely sound for existential statements | $T$ does not prove $\neg G$ |
| consistent, using Rosser's sentence in place of $G$ | $T$ proves neither the sentence nor its negation |

The first row is short. If $T$ proved $G$, the proof would have a code, $T$
would verify that code and so prove $\operatorname{Prov}_T(\ulcorner
G\urcorner)$, and by the equivalence also $\neg G$. That contradicts
consistency.

The second row is where the reconstructed reply in the discussion compresses
too much, with its phrase "the usual hypotheses". Consistency alone does not
stop $T$ from proving $\neg G$: a consistent theory can wrongly "believe" that
a proof exists while no actual number codes one. Gödel assumed
$\omega$-consistency to exclude this. Rosser removed the assumption in 1936 by
changing the sentence. The [[rosser-trick]] diagonalizes on "if there is a
proof of me, there is a shorter proof of my negation", and for that sentence
consistency suffices in both directions.

Three properties place the sentence among the concepts of this vault.

- **It is true if $T$ is consistent.** $G$ is equivalent to a statement of the
  form "no number codes a proof of $G$", and the first row says that statement
  holds. This is seen from outside $T$, in a theory that assumes $T$'s
  consistency. It is not a proof in $T$.
- **It is relative to $T$.** There is no single Gödel sentence. Add $G$ to $T$
  as an axiom and the new theory has its own provability predicate and its
  own undecided sentence. The discussion builds a whole sequence on this, the
  [[reflection-progression]].
- **It is where diagonalization and the strange loop coincide.** Escher's
  hands and Bach's canon cross levels without any diagonal lemma. Cantor's
  argument diagonalizes without any self-reference. The Gödel sentence is in
  the overlap: a level-crossing loop realised by a formal diagonal fixed
  point.

The loop has the three ingredients of Hofstadter's definition. The levels are
numbers and statements about proofs. The crossing is [[godel-numbering]]. The
return is the fixed point, which makes a statement about proofs be a
statement about one particular number, its own code.

## Relations

- It is the canonical formal [[strange-loop]] {relation: example-of}.
- It is the instance of the [[diagonal-lemma]] {relation: instance-of} for the property "not provable".
- It is a [[fixed-point]] {relation: instance-of} of negated provability.
- It rests on [[godel-numbering]] {relation: uses} for its crossing of levels.
- It is the witness in the first of the [[incompleteness-theorems]] {relation: part-of}.
- It is an independent sentence in the first sense of [[undecidability]] {relation: example-of}.
- It is the axiom added at each stage of a [[reflection-progression]] {relation: part-of}.
- It is refined by the [[rosser-trick]] {relation: generalizes}, which needs only consistency.
- It is not a [[liar-paradox]] {relation: distinguished-from}: it speaks of provability, which is definable in arithmetic, and not of truth, which is not.
- It is the formal [[self-reference]] {relation: example-of} that has a provable consequence.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[diagonal-lemma]].** Every property of codes has a sentence asserting it
  of itself. The Gödel sentence is that lemma with unprovability as the
  property.
- **[[incompleteness-theorems]].** No consistent, effective, sufficiently
  strong theory decides every sentence of arithmetic. The Gödel sentence is
  the undecided sentence the proof exhibits.
- **[[reflection-progression]].** A sequence of theories each adding the
  previous one's Gödel sentence. It shows the sentence is a moving target, not
  a single gap to be filled.
- **[[diagonalization]].** A proof method that applies a family to its own
  index. Here it produces self-reference; in Cantor's hands it does not.

